            <ArticleTitle>Abdominal cocoon accompanied by multiple peritoneal loose body.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e6185</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/MD.0000000000006185</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="RATIONALE" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Abdominal cocoon and peritoneal loose body are both rare abdominal diseases.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENT CONCERNS" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">The patient reported in this case was a 47-year-old man who suffered from abdominal pain and distension for 3 days.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DIAGNOSIS" NlmCategory="METHODS">X-ray, computed tomography, and magnetic resonance imaging revealed multiple peritoneal loose body and small bowel obstruction, characterized by a total encapsulation of the small bowel with a fibrous membrane.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The patient underwent surgical treatment and exploratory laparotomy confirmed the diagnosis of abdominal cocoon.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OUTCOMES" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Histopathological examination of pelvic nodules confirmed peritoneal loose body.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="LESSONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">To our knowledge, the herein reported case is the first abdominal cocoon that was accompanied by multiple peritoneal loose body.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
